`Basic._eval_derivative_n_times` contains the following note:

https://github.com/sympy/sympy/blob/f3e579e008c38f316862f99f71ef9a0df35cd7f6/sympy/core/basic.py#L1967-L1973
For example, the n-th derivatives of $\sin$ and $\cos$ are well known:

$$
\frac{d^n}{dx^n}\sin x = \sin\left(x + \frac{\pi n}{2}\right)
$$

$$
\frac{d^n}{dx^n}\cos x = \cos\left(x + \frac{\pi n}{2}\right)
$$

Based on this, I believe it could be implemented as follows:

```python
class sin(TrigonometricFunction):
    ...
    def _eval_derivative_n_times(self, s, n):
        if self.args[0] == s and n.is_integer and n.is_nonnegative:
            if n.is_Integer:
                match n % 4:
                    case 0:
                        return sin(s)
                    case 1:
                        return cos(s)
                    case 2:
                        return -sin(s)
                    case 3:
                        return -cos(s)
            return sin(s + pi*n/2)
        return super()._eval_derivative_n_times(s, n)
```

Would implementing such a trivial fact be considered unnecessary?

